foreign  [Music]  for Wednesday January 11th I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  the state of New York plans a major  shift to boost the number of electric  vehicles on its roads ears Governor  Kathy hokel at the New York  International Auto Show last year  finally we stopped this war between our  vehicle transportation and an assault on  the environment with leaning hard into  electric vehicles and that is why by the  year 2035  100 of new vehicles sold in the state of  New York will be zero emissions we are  leading the nation my friends and it's  starting right here in the state of New  York and I'm very very proud of that New  York's plan to ban gas vehicles follows  a precedent set by California to do the  same with the aim of cutting greenhouse  gas emissions but one particular  challenge for New York will be drivers  in New York City where many people rely  on street parking and there aren't all  that many places to put charging ports  with me to talk about this is wsj energy  reporter Jennifer Hiller hi Jennifer  thanks for joining me thanks for having  me I want to just start by laying out  the scale of this challenge I mean can  you tell us how many drivers now in New  York City have electric cars and how  many places are there to actually charge  them so fewer than one percent of  drivers in New York City have EVS it's  you know 20 something thousand drivers  so it's a small share of the drivers  that are out there and there's also not  a lot of charging locations at this  point there's about 1300 pieces of  equipment out there that would be  available to all kinds of EV drivers to  public charge over a period of several  hours and then there's about 74 fast  Chargers and that's coming from State  data and from the Department of energy  which tracks EV Chargers and from a  atlas public policy which is an EV data  company  okay so they're going to need a lot more  Chargers to meet this you know change  that New York is hoping to get when it  stops allowing the sale of new gas  powered vehicles and Jennifer full  disclosure I lived in New York City I  had a car when I lived in New York City  which I had to park on the street as  most people there do it's hard to  imagine where I would have charged it if  it was electric where are they planning  on putting these chargers right the city  estimates they have an enormous need for  public Chargers just because of the  thing that you mentioned most people in  New York are parking on the street they  don't have dedicated garage spaces where  they can leave their cars overnight and  so in New York probably more so than any  other city in the U.S by far you need a  lot of public Chargers available to  people and so those are going to be in  parking garages and you're also going to  have them essentially out on the street  they're looking at doing a lot of  curbside charging in neighborhoods and  the idea being that if they put enough  carbside Chargers out there that people  will be able to find a place to plug in  during the day or overnight but it is an  enormous infrastructure build out that  they are talking about doing the city  thinks it needs about 40  000 slower Chargers which are the kind  that maybe you plug in over a period of  you know six eight hours or more and  they want about 6 000 fast Chargers so  that is just a far cry from what exists  right now in the city you know Jennifer  real estate in New York is so expensive  how do they plan to get the space to  install these Chargers yeah that's one  of the big challenges for people who are  developing these sites you know real  estate is costly inexpensive and in New  York is not an easy City to build in or  to add any kind of infrastructure so the  permitting process can be complicated  you have to go through a process with  the utility as well and that can be  complicated hated and so you know New  York is an expensive place to add this  kind of equipment there is a lot of  State money on the table there's a lot  of federal money on the table that can  help offset some of the cost of  installing this kind of equipment but it  is not by any stretch of the imagination  and easy thing for people to build right  now it takes a lot of electricity  industry expertise and it also takes a  lot of real estate industry expertise to  get a project like this done I think  especially on the fast Chargers which  require quite a lot of power you know a  large fast charging site can draw as  much power as a skyscraper so it's not  just adding a few pieces of equipment  and plugging them into the existing  system and you're done you're talking  about utility upgrades that need to  happen as well right where is all that  extra power going to be coming from so  some companies are essentially drawing  it from the buildings that are already  there because the power system for the  building exists and depending on you  know how you manage the charging and you  manage the power use of the building  there might be enough power there  already otherwise you know it's coming  from the grid and so that could be an  issue over the years as you start to add  more and more charges in New York or in  cities across the country that it's a  new use of electricity that we don't  have right now on that kind of scale you  know most experts seem to think that  with managed charging you will be fine  and that there is enough power there but  I guess time will tell and see if they  run into any hiccups and adding this  kind of equipment I imagine this isn't  just a New York problem won't other  cities face similar challenges when they  try to make a shift towards more UVS  yeah I think most of our you know large  cities in the US have dense Urban cores  but I do think New York is unique in  some ways I mean it is going to have far  more people that are going to to rely on  public charging than other cities in the  U.S and you're just talking about like  the kind of density that we really don't  have anywhere else in the country at  least not on this scale so it is unique  a little bit in that way it's hard to  find kind of a one-to-one comparison for  New York but at the same time the same  things that New York is dealing with are  the same issues that are going to play  out in other cities across the country  all right that's our energy reporter  Jennifer Hiller Jennifer thanks for  joining us thanks so much for having me  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing for more Tech stories check out  our website wsj.com  I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall Street  Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

